  "response": "Rival readings of a burial site are adjudicated less by appeal to interpretive taste than by the systematic reconstruction of how the deposit came to be as it is, and both parties are in the first instance committed to the same inferential framework. Taphonomy is the study of the processes that affect remains between death and discovery, and this concern with everything that intervenes between the two has led anthropologists to compare the observed positions of skeletal elements against the expected anatomical arrangement of an intact body. The outcome of setting observed positions against the expected anatomical arrangement is what confirms that retention of articulation at labile joints such as the hands and vertebrae indicates rapid burial of a fleshed body. In the case at issue, that comparison led to the finding that the skeletal elements retained their original anatomical relationships to one another; that is, the bones lay in articulated positions matching the layout of the body at death.\n\nThe competing hypothesis must be pressed with equal rigour, by specifying in advance what a purely natural agency would have been expected to leave behind. Fluvial transport sorts disarticulated skeletal elements according to their size and density, and that sorting action is what has resulted in the preferred orientation of long bones along a single axis being used as an indicator of water flow across a deposit. Whereas that mode of sorting keys on the size and density of the elements, Voorhies groups classify skeletal elements according to their susceptibility to carnivore chewing. Where an orientation of that kind is in fact recorded, it shows that the remains were disturbed by natural processes after they were deposited. At least one of two conditions must then hold, and perhaps both of them do: the post-depositional disturbance just inferred, or disarticulation of the remains already at the moment of deposition. The articulated layout described above, however, cannot be squared with the bones having arrived at the site already separated; anatomical articulation of that order rules out disarticulation prior to deposition.\n\nThe procedure is visible in the most closely watched case of the past decade. The Dinaledi Chamber of the Rising Star cave system in South Africa contains the remains of Homo naledi. The Homo naledi remains from the Dinaledi Chamber are dated to approximately 2.5 million years ago. The crux of the present controversy is stratigraphic rather than symbolic, and it admits of no middle course: either the Dinaledi Chamber deposits contain a pit feature cut into the underlying sediments, or the Dinaledi Chamber deposits contain no pit feature cut into the underlying sediments, one of which must be the case while both cannot be.\n\nAdjudication therefore turns on which account predicts the finer texture of the assemblage, joint by joint and element by element, and on whether the sedimentary matrix independently corroborates the sequence of events that an intentional reading requires. Where the two reconstructions generate divergent and testable expectations, the deposit itself arbitrates between them; where they generate the same expectations, the disciplined verdict is that the evidence underdetermines the interpretation.",
